---
import ShakespeareLayout from '@/layouts/ShakespeareLayout.astro';
import Concept from '@/components/concept/Concept.astro';
---

<ShakespeareLayout
  title="Creating Fearless Shakespeare Scripts"
  subPage="alternatives"
  eyebrow="Alternatives to a full play"
  description="Four proven alternatives to staging a full Shakespeare play &mdash; the organizing logic behind DT:FC&rsquo;s Shakespeare libraries."
>
  <div class="max-w-2xl space-y-12">

    <section id="promise">
      <p class="text-ink-700 text-lg leading-relaxed">
        Shakespeare entertains and educates &mdash; in his time and 440+ years later. We have
        created, and you can create, alternatives to performing a whole Shakespeare play. We
        recommend alternative scripts when a whole play takes too long or doesn&rsquo;t involve
        all available Players. Example scripts are provided throughout our libraries.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="doctrine">
      <h2>Leave the Language as Shakespeare&rsquo;s Own</h2>
      <p class="text-ink-700 leading-relaxed">
        If you are thinking Shakespeare&rsquo;s language is too difficult to speak, remember he
        wrote in a period of history where many people did not read &mdash; his plays are part of
        an oral tradition. It&rsquo;s almost by chance that we have 37 of them. We leave the
        language as it is because when spoken the rhythm and content will carry the meaning. Read
        the plays aloud, even if by yourself. Be fearless.
      </p>
      <p class="text-ink-700 mt-4 leading-relaxed">
        When novice Players, including grade-schoolers, have permission to play with the language
        they naturally learn diction, voice intonation, and breath support.
      </p>
      <blockquote class="border-clay-500 mt-6 border-l-2 pl-4 italic text-ink-800">
        &ldquo;What translation did you use?&rdquo;
        <footer class="text-ink-600 mt-1 text-sm not-italic">
          &mdash; a repeated question from teachers after watching Players do the real thing.
          Answer: none.
        </footer>
      </blockquote>
      <div class="callout-tip mt-6">
        Use <Concept id="vocal-expression" /> Theatre Games as <Concept id="warmup" /> before
        Shakespeare rehearsal.
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="alternative-one">
      <h2>Alternative One &mdash; Scenes</h2>
      <p class="text-ink-700 leading-relaxed">
        A scene is a piece of a play that happens in a specific place at a specific time. It can
        involve one or more characters. Players learn one scene instead of a whole play &mdash; ten
        people doing a two-person scene opens a door to five different interpretations!
      </p>
      <p class="text-ink-700 mt-4 leading-relaxed">
        A brilliant and fun use of scenes: elementary school teachers in Colorado worked with the
        University of Colorado&rsquo;s Will Power programme so that different classes in different
        schools each learned a different scene of the same play. Then, on a spring day, everyone
        came to CU&rsquo;s Mary Rippon Theatre and performed their scene. The Players watched the
        other schools&rsquo; scenes in order and saw how their own scene fit into the
        storyline. <span class="text-ink-500 text-sm">(Will Power article&nbsp;<span class="bg-clay-100 text-clay-700 rounded px-1 py-0.5 text-xs font-medium">pending</span>)</span>
      </p>
      <p class="text-ink-700 mt-4 leading-relaxed">
        A similar alternative is a &ldquo;string of beads&rdquo; &mdash; scenes strung together by
        narration that fills in the spaces between the chosen scenes. Some examples:
      </p>
      <ol class="text-ink-700 mt-3 list-decimal space-y-2 pl-6">
        <li>
          <strong>Falstaff</strong> &mdash; telling the story of the character using scenes from
          the History plays and <em>Merry Wives of Windsor</em>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Romeo and Juliet</strong> scenes with narration filling in the story. The Nurse
          is a favored character as narrator.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A Midsummer Night&rsquo;s Dream</strong> with the scenes being the Lovers
          and/or the
          <a href="/shakespeare/scripts/mechanicals-scenes-a-midsummer-nights-dream/">
            Mechanicals &rarr;
          </a>
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p class="mt-6">
        <a href="/shakespeare/scenes/" class="hover:text-clay-500 font-medium">Browse the Scenes library &rarr;</a>
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="alternative-two">
      <h2>Alternative Two &mdash; Scenes Around a Theme</h2>
      <p class="text-ink-700 leading-relaxed">
        A theme is chosen from seemingly inexhaustible possibilities given the breadth and depth of
        Shakespeare&rsquo;s plays. Scenes from various plays &mdash; and even language from the
        Sonnets &mdash; are woven together in a sequence that explores the theme. There might be an
        introduction and short &ldquo;bridges&rdquo; between scenes, but we find them unnecessary.
      </p>
      <p class="text-ink-700 mt-4 leading-relaxed">
        Scripts can be weighted with serious material or have a more humorous, riotous, even
        uproarious set of scenes. Shakespeare often strikes a balance with both in one play.
        Consider the audience who will be viewing the production.
      </p>
      <p class="text-ink-700 mt-4 leading-relaxed">
        Sometimes the arrangement of scenes is obvious; other times it takes careful exploration.
      </p>
      <blockquote class="border-clay-500 mt-4 border-l-2 pl-4 italic text-ink-800">
        Experiment. Shakespeare did.
      </blockquote>
      <p class="text-ink-700 mt-4 font-medium">Themes we&rsquo;ve worked with:</p>
      <ol class="text-ink-700 mt-2 list-decimal space-y-1 pl-6">
        <li>Battle of the Sexes</li>
        <li>Magic and the Supernatural</li>
        <li>Fools and Fooling</li>
        <li>Ruler and the Ruled</li>
        <li>Rogues and Villains</li>
        <li>The Generation Gap</li>
        <li>Bullies (CU)</li>
        <li>Falstaff</li>
      </ol>
      <p class="text-ink-700 mt-4 leading-relaxed">
        We perform Scenes Around a Theme without narration. Changes in Players or an added hat or
        headscarf may indicate a different setting or a different play &mdash; but hints like this
        are not necessary. If knowing which play is desired, very short narrative pieces or an
        easel with signboards listing the play and scene will serve.
      </p>
      <div class="callout-tradeoffs mt-6">
        <p>
          When you assemble scenes around a theme, you are working with a narrator function &mdash;
          a &ldquo;string of beads&rdquo; structure. Your audience sees a set of scenes that make
          a kind of sense together, a shape that doesn&rsquo;t quite exist in
          Shakespeare&rsquo;s own lexicon. That&rsquo;s the trade-off you accept for the
          thematic depth this format offers.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p class="mt-6">
        <a href="/shakespeare/themes/" class="hover:text-clay-500 font-medium">Browse the Themes library &rarr;</a>
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="alternative-three">
      <h2>Alternative Three &mdash; Cuttings</h2>
      <p class="text-ink-700 leading-relaxed">
        Cut the three-plus-hour play to forty minutes (or twenty). Here&rsquo;s the method:
      </p>
      <ol class="text-ink-700 mt-3 list-decimal space-y-2 pl-6">
        <li>
          Begin by recording how you and/or others tell the play to someone else in three minutes.
        </li>
        <li>
          Find and read through a summary of the play (available on various websites and in books).
        </li>
        <li>List the scenes you will use.</li>
        <li>
          Then &mdash; mercilessly cut the scenes internally. Create a story line that gives
          character opportunities to your Players.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p class="text-ink-700 mt-4 leading-relaxed">
        Chuck Wilcox is providing plays he cut for use while teaching elementary grades at
        St. Mary&rsquo;s Academy in Denver. More scripts are being prepared over time.
      </p>
      <div class="callout-tradeoffs mt-6">
        <p>
          A cutting creates yet another reality that is not truly Shakespeare &mdash; whole
          sections of the play are left out. That&rsquo;s the trade-off you accept for a
          forty-minute playable version that keeps the story intact.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p class="mt-6">
        <a href="/shakespeare/cuttings/" class="hover:text-clay-500 font-medium">Browse the Cuttings library &rarr;</a>
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="alternative-four">
      <h2>Alternative Four &mdash; New Plays with a Shakespeare Foundation</h2>
      <p class="text-ink-700 leading-relaxed">
        It is possible to lift a play&rsquo;s storyline, characters, and even dialogue into a
        different script. The themes are universal; the characters are archetypal. The language is
        modernized or adapted to a different dialect altogether. <em>West Side Story</em> &mdash;
        with music by Leonard Bernstein and book by Arthur Laurents &mdash; is the major example,
        transplanting <em>Romeo and Juliet</em>&rsquo;s scaffolding into 1950s New York.
      </p>
      <p class="text-ink-700 mt-4 leading-relaxed">
        There are endless possibilities to take a play, or Shakespeare himself, and create a piece
        that provides both entertainment and exploratory knowledge.
      </p>
      <div class="callout-tradeoffs my-6 p-4">
        <p>
          A Shakespeare-inspired new play is Shakespeare-adjacent, not truly
          Shakespeare. When purists ask, we answer: yes, it walks in his footsteps,
          and it invites Players who a whole play might exclude.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p class="text-ink-700 mt-4 font-medium">Two DT:FC-family new plays:</p>
      <ul class="text-ink-700 mt-3 list-none space-y-4 pl-0">
        <li>
          <strong><em>The Ballad of Three Finger Dick</em></strong> by Chuck Wilcox &mdash;
          set in the American Wild West, using the language and idiom of that time and place.
          <a href="/shakespeare/honoring-our-guides/#chuck-wilcox" class="hover:text-clay-500 ml-1 text-sm">Chuck&rsquo;s story on Honoring Our Guides &rarr;</a>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong><em>Shakespeare&rsquo;s Sister</em></strong> by Marta Barnard &mdash; written
          for two women. When a Player fell out the day before opening, Marta performed both roles
          solo &mdash; the two-woman script became a <strong>last-minute</strong>
          one-woman show, and stayed that way for the tour.
          <a href="/shakespeare/honoring-our-guides/#marta-barnard" class="hover:text-clay-500 ml-1 text-sm">Marta&rsquo;s story on Honoring Our Guides &rarr;</a>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-6">
        <a href="/shakespeare/new-plays/" class="hover:text-clay-500 font-medium">Browse the New Plays library &rarr;</a>
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="contact">
      <div class="callout-tip">
        <strong>Want to see a script?</strong> Reach out through
        <a href="/shakespeare/ask-shakespeare/#form" class="hover:text-clay-700 font-medium">Ask Shakespeare</a>
        &mdash; we&rsquo;re glad to share cuts, scenes, and works-in-progress.
      </div>
    </section>

  </div>
</ShakespeareLayout>
